helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150147				0	uz	For example, to hide a paragraph, text, or a section from a user with a specific initial, such as "LM", enter the condition: user_initials=="LM".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154115				0	uz	Conditions and Database Fields				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154128				0	uz	You can define conditions for accessing databases, or database fields. For example, you can check the contents of a database field from a condition, or use database fields in logical expressions. The following table lists a few more examples of using databases in conditions:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156066				0	uz	Example				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156088				0	uz	Meaning				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155948				0	uz	Database.Table.Company				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3148673				0	uz	Database.Table.Company NEQ ""				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3148687				0	uz	Database.Table.Company != ""				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155373				0	uz	The condition is true if the COMPANY field is not empty. (In the first example, no operator is required.)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149977				0	uz	!Database.Table.Company				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149991				0	uz	NOT Database.Table.Company				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150004				0	uz	Database.Table.Company EQ ""				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3146911				0	uz	Database.Table.Company ==""				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3148762				0	uz	Database.Table.Company ==""				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153016				0	uz	Database.Table.Company NEQ ""				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154605				0	uz	Database.Table.Firstname AND Database.Table.Name				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153059				0	uz	Returns TRUE if the record contains the first and the last name.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3159247				0	uz	Note the difference between the boolean NOT "!" and the comparative operator not equal "!=" (NEQ).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153876				0	uz	When you refer to a database field in a condition, use the form Databasename.Tablename.Fieldname. If one of the names contains a character that is an operator, such as a minus sign (-), enclose the name in square brackets, for example, Databasename.[Table-name].Fieldname. Never use spaces inside field names.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153891				0	uz	Misol: Boʻsh maʼlumot bazasi maydonini bekitish				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150051				0	uz	You may want to create a condition that hides an empty field, for example, if the COMPANY field is empty for some of the data records.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150067				0	uz	Select the \<emph\>Hidden Paragraph\</emph\> box, and type the following condition: Addressbook.Addresses.Company EQ ""				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147110				0	uz	yoki quyidagini kiriting				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147123				0	uz	NOT Addressbook.Addresses.Company				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147136				0	uz	If the COMPANY database field is empty, the condition is true and the paragraph is hidden.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145218				0	uz	Examples of Conditions in Fields				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145231				0	uz	The following examples use the Conditional text field, although they can be applied to any fields that can be linked to a condition. The syntax used for conditions is also used for the Hidden text, Hidden paragraph, Any record or Next record fields.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150311				0	uz	To display conditional text based on the number of pages:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150333				0	uz	Choose \<emph\>Insert - Fields - Other\</emph\>, and then click the \<emph\>Functions\</emph\> tab.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147471				0	uz	In the \<emph\>Type\</emph\> list, click "Conditional text".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
