helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154840				0	uz	\<link href=\"text/shared/autopi/01110200.xhp\" name=\"HTML Export - Page 2\"\>HTML Export - Page 2\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154094				0	uz	Determines the type of publication.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151330				0	uz	You can specify if you want to include frames, create a title, or display presentation notes.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152924				0	uz	Publication type				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154751				0	uz	Defines the basic settings for the intended export.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147399				0	uz	Standard HTML format				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147088				0	uz	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:RADIOBUTTON:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE2_STANDARD\"\>Creates standard HTML pages from export pages.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145071				0	uz	Standard HTML with frames				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154824				0	uz	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:RADIOBUTTON:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE2_FRAMES\"\>Creates standard HTML pages with frames. The exported page will be placed in the main frame, and the frame to the left will display a table of contents in the form of hyperlinks.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149398				0	uz	Create title page				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3152780				0	uz	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:CHECKBOX:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE2_CONTENT\"\>Creates a title page for your document.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3163804				0	uz	\<switchinline select=\"appl\"\>\<caseinline select=\"IMPRESS\"\>Show notes \</caseinline\>\</switchinline\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3157909				0	uz	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:CHECKBOX:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE2_NOTES\"\>Specifies that your notes are also displayed.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156117				0	uz	Automatic				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149233				0	uz	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:RADIOBUTTON:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE2_KIOSK\"\>Creates a default HTML presentation as a kiosk export, in which the slides are automatically advanced after a specified amount of time.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155421				0	uz	As stated in document				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150275				0	uz	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:RADIOBUTTON:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE2_CHG_DEFAULT\"\>The slide transition depends on the timing that you set for each slide in the presentation. If you set a manual page transition, the HTML presentation introduces a new page by pressing any key from your keyboard.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156307				0	uz	Automatic				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153126				0	uz	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:RADIOBUTTON:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE2_CHG_AUTO\"\>The page transition takes place automatically after the specified period of time elapses and does not depend on the presentation's contents\</ahelp\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150503				0	uz	Slide view time				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151245				0	uz	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:TIMEFIELD:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE2_DURATION_TMF\"\>Defines the amount of time for each slide display.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154347				0	uz	Endless				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149655				0	uz	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:CHECKBOX:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE2_ENDLESS\"\>Automatically restarts the HTML presentation after the last slide has been displayed.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151054				0	uz	WebCast				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150543				0	uz	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:RADIOBUTTON:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE2_WEBCAST\"\>In a WebCast export, automatic scripts will be generated with Perl or ASP support.\</ahelp\> This enables the speaker (for example, a speaker in a telephone conference using a slide show on the Internet) to change the slides in the audience's web browsers. You will find more information on \<link href=\"text/shared/autopi/01110200.xhp\" name=\"WebCast\"\>WebCast\</link\> later in this section.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154365				0	uz	Active Server Pages (ASP)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3148922	28			0	uz	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:RADIOBUTTON:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE2_ASP\"\>When you select the\<emph\> ASP \</emph\>option, the WebCast export creates ASP pages. Note that the HTML presentation can only be offered by a web server supporting ASP.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149765				0	uz	Perl				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145174				0	uz	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:RADIOBUTTON:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE2_PERL\"\>Used by WebCast export to create HTML pages and Perl scripts.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150868				0	uz	URL for listeners				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149203				0	uz	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:EDIT:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE2_INDEX\"\>Specifies the URL (absolute or relative) to be entered by the viewer in order to see the presentation.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147228				0	uz	URL for presentation				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156214				0	uz	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:EDIT:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE2_URL\"\>Specifies the URL (absolute or relative), where the created HTML presentation on the web server has been saved.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153367				0	uz	URL for Perl scripts				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3159255				0	uz	\<ahelp hid=\"SD:EDIT:DLG_PUBLISHING:PAGE2_CGI\"\>Specifies the URL (absolute or relative) for the generated Perl scripts.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150486				0	uz	More Information on WebCast Export				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3152578				0	uz	There are two possible options for exporting $[officename] Impress presentations using WebCast technology: Active Server Pages (ASP) and Perl.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153364				0	uz	In either case, the WebCast needs an HTTP server offering either Perl or ASP as scripting. Therefore, the exporting option depends on the HTTP server used.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151112				0	uz	WebCast in ASP				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159197				0	uz	Exporting				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3146119				0	uz	To export to ASP, in a $[officename] Impress document choose \<emph\>File - Export\</emph\>. You then see the \<emph\>Export\</emph\> dialog in which you select \<emph\>HTML Document\</emph\> as the file type. Once you have selected a directory and entered a file name, click \<emph\>Save\</emph\>. For export as ASP, we recommend selecting a "secret" file name for the HTML file (see below for more details). You then see the \<emph\>HTML Export\</emph\> dialog. Several files will be written to the directory you have just selected.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149410				0	uz	The presenter uses the entered file name to change between the slides viewed by the audience. You can save the WebCast files locally or save them directly to an HTTP server. You can later transfer locally saved files to the HTTP server by FTP. Note that WebCast only works if the files are requested over an HTTP server.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3157974				0	uz	Do not use the same directory for two different HTML exports.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153157				0	uz	Select \<emph\>WebCast\</emph\> as a publishing type on the second page of the HTML Export Wizard.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154790				0	uz	In the options area for WebCast, select the \<emph\>Active Server Pages (ASP)\</emph\> option. You can now continue defining other settings or start the export by clicking the \<emph\>Create\</emph\> button.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153281				0	uz	Using ASP WebCast				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149910				0	uz	You can use WebCast as soon as the exported files can be accessed from an HTTP server.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154503				0	uz	\<emph\>Example\</emph\>:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3152375				0	uz	Let's assume that you installed the Microsoft Internet Information Server on your computer. You entered the "c:\\Inet\\wwwroot\\presentation" directory as an HTML output directory during the IIS setup. The URL of your computer is assumed as follows: "http://myserver.com".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150715				0	uz	You have saved the files that have been created during the Export process in the c:\\Inet\\wwwroot\\presentation\\ directory. In this directory, the Export creates an HTML file that can be named, for example, as "secret.htm". You entered this name in the Save dialog (see above). The presenter can now browse to the HTML Export files by entering the http://myserver.com/presentation/secret.htm URL in any HTTP Browser having JavaScript support. The presenter is now able to modify the page using some form controls.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3159110				0	uz	The audience can now view the slide selected by the presenter through the URL http://myserver.com/presentation/webcast.asp. They cannot move to other slides found at this URL, unless the file names are known. Please ensure that the HTTP server does not show the directory listing.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153732				0	uz	WebCast over Perl				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152999				0	uz	Exporting				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3146972				0	uz	To export, in a $[officename] Impress document choose \<emph\>File - Export\</emph\>. This opens the \<emph\>Export\</emph\> dialog, in which you select \<emph\>HTML Document\</emph\> as the file type. After selecting a folder and entering a file name, click \<emph\>Save\</emph\>. This opens the \<emph\>HTML Export Wizard\</emph\>. This will write some files to the folder you have just selected.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156362				0	uz	The entered file name will be used by the presenter to switch through the slides. Please select an empty directory.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3148432				0	uz	In the second page of the HTML Export, select \<emph\>WebCast\</emph\> as the publication type.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149018				0	uz	In the option area for WebCast, select \<emph\>Perl\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154509				0	uz	In the \<emph\>URL for listeners\</emph\> text box, enter the file name of the HTML document that will be used by the audience. In \<emph\>URL for presentation\</emph\>, enter the URL of the directory that will be used for the presentation and, in \<emph\>URL for Perl scripts\</emph\>, enter the URL for the CGI script directory. You can now define further settings on the following pages of the Wizard or start the export process by clicking the \<emph\>Create\</emph\> button.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148996				0	uz	Using Perl WebCast				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3159268				0	uz	The files that have been created during the export must now be set up in the Perl enabled HTTP server. This cannot be done automatically because of the variety of different HTTP servers having Perl support. The steps to follow will be described next. Please refer to your server manual or ask your network administrator how to apply these steps on your server.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147340				0	uz	You should first move the files that have been created during the export into the correct directory on the HTTP server.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155936				0	uz	Move all files having the htm, jpg and gif extensions into the directory on your HTTP server that has been referred to in the text box \<emph\>URL for presentation\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151014				0	uz	All files having the pl and txt extensions have to be moved into the directory on your HTTP server that has been referred to in the \<emph\>URL for Perl scripts\</emph\> text box. This directory has to be configured in a way that the Perl scripts contained there can also be run by an HTTP request.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151250				0	uz	On UNIX systems grant the files with the pl extension the rights to be executable by the HTTP server. Normally, this is done with the chmod command. The rights of the currpic.txt file must be set to be writable by the HTTP server.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153534				0	uz	Now you should be able to use WebCast.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156711	66			0	uz	\<emph\>Example\</emph\>:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147313				0	uz	In this example, you have a Linux computer with an HTTP server. The URL of your HTTP server is http://myserver.com and the output directory of your HTML documents is the //user/local/http/ directory. Your Perl scripts are contained in the //user/local/http/cgi-bin/ directory. Enter secret.htm as an export file name and presentation.htm as \<emph\>URL for listeners\</emph\>. In the \<emph\>URL for presentation\</emph\> text box enter http://myserver.com/presentation/ and for the \<emph\>URL for Perl scripts\</emph\> enter http://myserver.com/cgi-bin/.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145792				0	uz	Now, copy all *.htm, *.jpg and *.gif files from the directories that were specified during the export into the //user/local/http/presentation/ directory on your HTTP Server and copy all files with the *.pl and *.txt extensions into the //user/local/http/cgi-bin/ directory.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153920				0	uz	Login on your server as root and switch to the //user/local/http/cgi-bin/ directory. You can define the corresponding rights using the chmod command.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01110200.xhp	0	help	par_id3148479				0	uz	Once you have finished installing the Perl files, the presenter will be able to give the presentation. The listeners can view this presentation under the URL http://myserver.com/presentation/presentation.htm.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01010000.xhp	0	help	tit				0	uz	Letter Wizard				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01010000.xhp	0	help	bm_id3151100				0	uz	\<bookmark_value\>wizards; letters\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>Letter Wizard\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>templates;letters\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01010000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151100				0	uz	Letter Wizard				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01010000.xhp	0	help	par_id3093440				0	uz	\<variable id=\"brief\"\>\<ahelp hid=\".uno:AutoPilotLetter\"\>Starts the wizard for a letter template.\</ahelp\>\</variable\> You can use this template for both business and personal correspondence.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01010000.xhp	0	help	par_id3149178				0	uz	$[officename] comes with sample templates for personal or business letters, which you can customize to your own needs with the help of the wizard. The wizard leads you step-by-step in creating a document template and offers numerous layout and design options. The preview gives you an impression of how the finished letter will appear according to the settings you choose.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01010000.xhp	0	help	par_id3153748				0	uz	Within the wizard, you can modify your entries and options at any time. You may also skip an entire page or even all the wizard pages, in which case the current (or default) settings will remain in effect.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01010000.xhp	0	help	par_id3153824				0	uz	If you are creating a business letter, you can select a variety of elements to include in your document, which usually do not apply to personal letters, such as a subject line. If you choose the \<emph\>Personal\</emph\> letter option, some pages which contain elements specific to business letters will not be included in the wizard dialog.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01010000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159176				0	uz	Back				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01010000.xhp	0	help	par_id3153543	7			0	uz	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_LTRWIZARD_BACK\"\>Allows you to view the selections that you made on the previous steps.\</ahelp\> The current settings will be saved. 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01010000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150254				0	uz	Next				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01010000.xhp	0	help	par_id3155923				0	uz	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_LTRWIZARD_NEXT\"\>Saves the current settings and continues to the next page.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01010000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148944				0	uz	Finish				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01010000.xhp	0	help	par_id3149669				0	uz	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_LTRWIZARD_CREATE\"\>According to your selections, the wizard creates a new document template and saves it on your hard disk.\</ahelp\> $[officename] creates a new document based on the existing templates with the "Untitled X" name (X stands for the consecutive numbering) and displays it on the work area.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\autopi\01010000.xhp	0	help	par_id3144433				0	uz	$[officename] saves the current settings in the wizard according to the chosen template. These settings are used as the default settings the next time you activate the wizard.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
