helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\navigator_setcursor.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150774				0	uz	\<bookmark_value\>Document Map, see Navigator\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cursor;quickly moving to an object\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>objects;quickly moving to\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>navigating;in documents\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>Navigator;working with\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\navigator_setcursor.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150774				0	uz	\<variable id=\"navigator_setcursor\"\>\<link href=\"text/shared/guide/navigator_setcursor.xhp\" name=\"Navigation to Quickly Reach Objects\"\>Navigation to Quickly Reach Objects\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\navigator_setcursor.xhp	0	help	par_id3145071				0	uz	This is a common use of the Navigator.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\navigator_setcursor.xhp	0	help	par_id3145382				0	uz	Double-click an object in the Navigator to jump directly to the position of the object in the document.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\navigator_setcursor.xhp	0	help	par_id3163802				0	uz	You can use the \<emph\>Navigation\</emph\> toolbar to scroll to the previous or next object of a specific category.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\navigator_setcursor.xhp	0	help	par_id3148491				0	uz	Open the toolbar using the \<emph\>Navigation\</emph\> icon below the vertical scroll bar of a text document, or in the Navigator window.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\navigator_setcursor.xhp	0	help	par_id3153348				0	uz	On the \<emph\>Navigation\</emph\> toolbar, you first select the category, then click on one of the buttons, \<emph\>Previous Object\</emph\> or \<emph\>Next Object\</emph\>. The names of the buttons refer to the category, for example, the button "Next Object" is named "Next Page" or "Next Bookmark" according to the category.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\autohide.xhp	0	help	tit				0	uz	Showing, Docking and Hiding Windows				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\autohide.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150713				0	uz	\<bookmark_value\>Gallery; hiding/showing\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>data source view; showing\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>Navigator; docking\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>Styles and Formatting window; docking\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>windows; hiding/showing/docking\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>docking; windows\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>undocking windows\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>showing;docked windows\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>hiding;docked windows\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\autohide.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145346				0	uz	\<variable id=\"autohide\"\>\<link href=\"text/shared/guide/autohide.xhp\" name=\"Showing, Docking and Hiding Windows\"\>Showing, Docking and Hiding Windows\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\autohide.xhp	0	help	par_id3147242				0	uz	Some windows in $[officename] are dockable, such as the Navigator window. You can move these windows, re-size them or dock them to an edge.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\autohide.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154750				0	uz	Docking and Undocking Windows				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\autohide.xhp	0	help	par_id3166460				0	uz	To dock a window, do one of the following:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\autohide.xhp	0	help	par_id3150503				0	uz	Drag the window by its title bar to the side, or				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\autohide.xhp	0	help	par_id3150275				0	uz	Double-click inside a vacant area of the window while holding down the Ctrl key. In the Styles and Formatting window, double-click a gray part of the window next to the icons while holding down the \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command \</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl \</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>key. Alternatively, press \<item type=\"keycode\"\>\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+Shift+F10\</item\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\autohide.xhp	0	help	par_id3147335				0	uz	These methods can also be used to undock a currently docked window.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\autohide.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149796				0	uz	Showing and Hiding Docked Windows				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\autohide.xhp	0	help	par_id3149045				0	uz	\<image id=\"img_id3149655\" src=\"res/helpimg/ein.png\" width=\"0.1043inch\" height=\"0.4272inch\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3149655\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\autohide.xhp	0	help	par_id3152921	64			0	uz	Click the button on the edge of the docked window to show or hide the docked window. The AutoHide function allows you to temporarily show a hidden window by clicking on its edge. When you click in the document, the docked window hides again. 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_title.xhp	0	help	tit				0	uz	Editing Chart Titles				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_title.xhp	0	help	bm_id3156136				0	uz	\<bookmark_value\>charts; editing titles\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>editing; chart titles\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>titles; editing in charts\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_title.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156136				0	uz	\<variable id=\"chart_title\"\>\<link href=\"text/shared/guide/chart_title.xhp\" name=\"Editing Chart Titles\"\>Editing Chart Titles\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_title.xhp	0	help	par_id3153527				0	uz	To edit a chart title that you have inserted into a $[officename] document:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_title.xhp	0	help	par_id3153681				0	uz	Double-click on the chart.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_title.xhp	0	help	par_id3145345				0	uz	A gray border appears around the chart and the menu bar now contains commands for editing the objects in the chart.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_title.xhp	0	help	par_id3149811				0	uz	Double-click on an existing title text. A gray border appears around the text and you can now make changes. Press Enter to create a new line.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_title.xhp	0	help	par_id2706991				0	uz	If no title text exists, choose \<emph\>Insert - Title\</emph\> to enter the text in a dialog.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_title.xhp	0	help	par_id3145382				0	uz	A single-click on the title allows you to move it with the mouse.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_title.xhp	0	help	par_id3155341				0	uz	If you want to change the formatting of the main title, choose \<emph\>Format - Title - Main Title\</emph\>. This opens the \<emph\>Title\</emph\> dialog.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_title.xhp	0	help	par_id3147336				0	uz	Select one of the available tabs in the dialog to make modifications.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_title.xhp	0	help	par_id3155135				0	uz	Click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>. In your document, click outside the chart to exit chart editing mode.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\chart_title.xhp	0	help	par_id3154046				0	uz	\<link href=\"text/schart/01/05010000.xhp\" name=\"Format - Object properties\"\>Format - Object properties\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\navigator.xhp	0	help	tit				0	uz	Navigator for Document Overview				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\navigator.xhp	0	help	bm_id3147008				0	uz	\<bookmark_value\>documents; contents as lists\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>Navigator; contents as lists\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\navigator.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147008				0	uz	\<variable id=\"navigator\"\>\<link href=\"text/shared/guide/navigator.xhp\" name=\"Navigator for Document Overview\"\>Navigator for Document Overview\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\navigator.xhp	0	help	par_id3154823				0	uz	All contents of the Navigator window are referred to here as "categories," whether titles, sheets, tables, text frames, graphics, OLE objects, sections, hyperlinks, references, indexes or notes.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\navigator.xhp	0	help	par_id3153662				0	uz	The Navigator displays all types of objects contained in a document. If a plus sign appears next to a category, this indicates that at least one object of this kind exists. If you rest the mouse pointer on the category name, the number of objects is displayed in an extended tip.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\navigator.xhp	0	help	par_id3146797				0	uz	Open a category by clicking on the plus sign. If you only want to view the entries in a certain category, select the category and click the \<emph\>Content View\</emph\> icon. Until you click the icon again, only the objects of this category will be displayed.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\navigator.xhp	0	help	par_id3166461				0	uz	You may dock the Navigator to any document border or turn it back into a free window (double click on the gray area). You can change the size of the Navigator when it is a free window.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\dragdrop_beamer.xhp	0	help	tit				0	uz	Drag-and-Drop With the Data Source View				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\dragdrop_beamer.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145071				0	uz	\<bookmark_value\>drag and drop; data source view\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>data source view; drag and drop\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>copying;from data source view\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>pasting;from data source view\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\dragdrop_beamer.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145071				0	uz	\<variable id=\"dragdrop_beamer\"\>\<link href=\"text/shared/guide/dragdrop_beamer.xhp\" name=\"Drag-and-Drop With the Data Source View\"\>Drag-and-Drop With the Data Source View\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\dragdrop_beamer.xhp	0	help	par_id3151111				0	uz	A fast way of copying from a data source into a text or spreadsheet document, or of creating forms based on a data source, is by drag-and-drop.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\dragdrop_beamer.xhp	0	help	par_id3147335				0	uz	\<image id=\"img_id3155390\" src=\"res/helpimg/copydata.png\" width=\"8.47mm\" height=\"8.47mm\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3155390\"\>Mouse pointer copying data\</alt\>\</image\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\dragdrop_beamer.xhp	0	help	par_id3145315				0	uz	Copying with Drag-and-Drop				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\dragdrop_beamer.xhp	0	help	par_id3149233				0	uz	If you want to reverse a drag-and-drop, position the cursor in your document and choose \<emph\>Edit - Undo\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\dragdrop_beamer.xhp	0	help	par_id3149656				0	uz	It is also possible to copy by drag-and-drop from a document into a data source:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\dragdrop_beamer.xhp	0	help	par_id3153379				0	uz	A text table or the selected range of a spreadsheet can be dragged using drag-and-drop to a table container in the data source explorer.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\dragdrop_beamer.xhp	0	help	par_id3151211				0	uz	Plain text can be copied using drag-and-drop from one document to a data field in the data source view.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\dragdrop_beamer.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145421				0	uz	Using data in a text document				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\dragdrop_beamer.xhp	0	help	par_id3154685				0	uz	You can insert a database field in a text document by dragging a field name from the column header of the data source view into the document. This is especially useful when designing form letters. Simply drag the desired fields - home address, form of address, and so on - into your document.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\dragdrop_beamer.xhp	0	help	par_id3153105				0	uz	To insert a complete record, select the corresponding header and drag it into the document. When you release the mouse button, the \<link href=\"text/shared/02/12070000.xhp\" name=\"Insert database columns\"\>\<emph\>Insert database columns\</emph\>\</link\> dialog appears, in which you can decide whether to use all database fields, and whether to copy the data into the document as text, a table or fields. All currently selected records will be inserted.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\dragdrop_beamer.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147230				0	uz	Applying data to a table document				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\dragdrop_beamer.xhp	0	help	par_id3125864				0	uz	You can insert one or more records into the current sheet of a spreadsheet by selecting the rows in the data source view and dragging and dropping them into the spreadsheet. The data is inserted at the place where you release the mouse button.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\dragdrop_beamer.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149766				0	uz	Inserting controls in a text form				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\dragdrop_beamer.xhp	0	help	par_id3155132				0	uz	When you create a text form linked to a database, you can generate controls by drag-and-drop from the data source view.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\dragdrop_beamer.xhp	0	help	par_id3149562				0	uz	When you drag a database column into the text document, you insert a field. If you hold down Shift+\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> while dragging, a text field is inserted, grouped with an appropriate label field. The text field already contains all the database information that you need for the form.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\startcenter.xhp	0	help	tit				0	uz	Start Center				2002-02-02 02:02:02
