helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\11020000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153665				0	uz	Password required				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\11020000.xhp	0	help	par_id3145119				0	uz	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Prevents an unauthorized user from accessing the database. You only need to enter the password once per session.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\11020000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153087				0	uz	Driver Settings				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\11020000.xhp	0	help	par_id3143271				0	uz	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_DSADMIN_ODBC_OPTIONS\"\>Use this text field to enter additional optional driver settings if this is necessary.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\11020000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152472				0	uz	Character Set				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\11020000.xhp	0	help	par_id3151245				0	uz	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_DSADMIN_CHARSET_ODBC\"\>Select the code conversion that you want to use to view the database in $[officename]. This does not affect the database.\</ahelp\> Choose "System" to use the default character set of your operating system. Text and dBASE databases are restricted to character sets with a fixed-size character length, where all characters are encoded with the same number of bytes.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\11020000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149669				0	uz	General				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\11020000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147265				0	uz	Retrieve generated values				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\11020000.xhp	0	help	par_id3151054				0	uz	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_DSADMIN_AUTORETRIEVEENABLED\"\>Enables $[officename] support of auto-incremented data fields for the current ODBC or JDBC data source.\</ahelp\> Select this check box if the database does not support the auto-increment feature in its SDBCX layer. In general, the auto-increment is selected for the primary key field.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\11020000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150400				0	uz	Auto-increment statement				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\11020000.xhp	0	help	par_id3154366	27			0	uz	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_DSADMIN_AUTOINCREMENTVALUE\"\>Enter the SQL command specifier that instructs the data source to auto-increment a specified Integer data field.\</ahelp\> For example, a typical SQL statement to create a data field is: 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\11020000.xhp	0	help	par_id3159149				0	uz	CREATE TABLE "table1" ("id" INTEGER)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\11020000.xhp	0	help	par_id3147084				0	uz	To auto-increment the "id" data field in a MySQL database, change the statement to:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\11020000.xhp	0	help	par_id3154909				0	uz	CREATE TABLE "table1" ("id" INTEGER AUTO_INCREMENT)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\11020000.xhp	0	help	par_id3152933				0	uz	In other words, enter AUTO_INCREMENT into \<emph\>Auto-increment statement\</emph\> box.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\11020000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149765				0	uz	Query of generated values				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\11020000.xhp	0	help	par_id3145171				0	uz	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_DSADMIN_RETRIEVE_AUTO\"\>Enter an SQL statement that returns the last auto-incremented value for the primary key data field.\</ahelp\> For example:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\11020000.xhp	0	help	par_id3150769				0	uz	SELECT LAST_INSERT_D();				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\11020000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3157892				0	uz	Use SQL92 naming constraints				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\11020000.xhp	0	help	par_id3153368				0	uz	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_DSADMIN_SQL92CHECK\"\>Only allows names that use characters that conform to the SQL92 naming constraints in the data source. All other characters are rejected.\</ahelp\> Each name must begin with a lower or upper case letter, or an underline ( _ ). The remaining characters can be ASCII letters, underlines, and numbers.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\11020000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154011				0	uz	Use Catalog for file-based databases				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\11020000.xhp	0	help	par_id3148618				0	uz	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_DSADMIN_USECATALOG\"\>Uses the current data source of the Catalog. This is useful when the ODBC data source is a database server. If the ODBC data source is a dBASE driver, leave this check box clear.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
