helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id372325				0	uz	A white line is shown when the corresponding line of the selected cells will be removed.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	hd_id7282937				0	uz	Examples				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id4230780				0	uz	Select a single cell, then choose \<emph\>Format - Cells - Borders\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id1712393				0	uz	Click the lower edge to set a very thin line as a lower border. All other lines will be removed from the cell.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id5149693				0	uz	\<image id=\"img_id9467452\" src=\"res/helpimg/border_ca_6.png\" width=\"1.2709inch\" height=\"1.1563inch\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id9467452\"\>setting a thin lower border\</alt\>\</image\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id5759453				0	uz	Choose a thicker line style and click the lower edge. This sets a thicker line as a lower border.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id6342051				0	uz	\<image id=\"img_id7431562\" src=\"res/helpimg/border_ca_7.png\" width=\"1.2811inch\" height=\"1.1354inch\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id7431562\"\>setting a thick line as a border\</alt\>\</image\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id5775322				0	uz	Click the second \<emph\>Default\</emph\> icon from the left to set all four borders. Then repeatedly click the lower edge until a white line is shown. This removes the lower border.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id2882778				0	uz	\<image id=\"img_id8155766.00000001\" src=\"res/helpimg/border_ca_8.png\" width=\"1.2709inch\" height=\"1.1354inch\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id8155766.00000001\"\>removing lower border\</alt\>\</image\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id8102053				0	uz	You can combine several line types and styles. The last image shows how to set thick outer borders (the thick black lines), while any diagonal lines inside the cell will not be touched (gray lines).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\borders.xhp	0	help	par_id2102420				0	uz	\<image id=\"img_id5380718\" src=\"res/helpimg/border_ca_9.png\" width=\"1.2709inch\" height=\"1.1563inch\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id5380718\"\>advanced example for cell borders\</alt\>\</image\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\year2000.xhp	0	help	tit				0	uz	19xx/20xx Years				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\year2000.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150439				0	uz	\<bookmark_value\>years; 2-digits\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>dates; 19xx/20xx\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\year2000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150439				0	uz	\<variable id=\"year2000\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/year2000.xhp\" name=\"19xx/20xx Years\"\>19xx/20xx Years\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\year2000.xhp	0	help	par_id3151116				0	uz	The year in a date entry is often entered as two digits. Internally, the year is managed by $[officename] as four digits, so that in the calculation of the difference from 1/1/99 to 1/1/01, the result will correctly be two years.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\year2000.xhp	0	help	par_id3150010				0	uz	This means that if you enter a date of 1/1/30 or higher, it will be treated internally as 1/1/1930 or higher. All lower two-digit years apply to the 20xx century. So, for example, 1/1/20 is converted into 1/1/2020.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\specialfilter.xhp	0	help	tit				0	uz	Filter: Applying Advanced Filters				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\specialfilter.xhp	0	help	bm_id3148798				0	uz	\<bookmark_value\>filters;defining advanced filters \</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>advanced filters\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>defining; advanced filters\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>database ranges; advanced filters\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\specialfilter.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148798	18			0	uz	\<variable id=\"specialfilter\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/specialfilter.xhp\" name=\"Filter: Applying Advanced Filters\"\>Filter: Applying Advanced Filters\</link\> \</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\specialfilter.xhp	0	help	par_id3145785				0	uz	Copy the column headers of the sheet ranges to be filtered into an empty area of the sheet, and then enter the criteria for the filter in a row beneath the headers. Horizontally arranged data in a row will always be logically connected with AND, and vertically arranged data in a column will always be logically connected with OR.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\specialfilter.xhp	0	help	par_id3153142				0	uz	Once you have created a filter matrix, select the sheet ranges to be filtered. Open the \<emph\>Advanced Filter\</emph\> dialog by choosing \<emph\>Data - Filter - Advanced Filter\</emph\>, and define the filter conditions.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\specialfilter.xhp	0	help	par_id3153726	21			0	uz	Then click OK, and you will see that only the rows from the original sheet whose contents have met the search criteria are still visible. All other rows are temporarily hidden and can be made to reappear with the \<emph\>Format - Row - Show \</emph\>command.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\specialfilter.xhp	0	help	par_id3149664	22			0	uz	 \<emph\>Example\</emph\> 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
