helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	tit				0	uz	Entering Text				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	bm_id3155962				0	uz	\<bookmark_value\>text strings; entering in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>direct text; entering in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>inserting;text in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	hd_id5676442				0	uz	\<variable id=\"text\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/text.xhp\" name=\"Entering Text\"\>Entering Text\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	hd_id8509170				0	uz	How to enter direct text strings that do not get interpreted?				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id337229				0	uz	Some text strings get interpreted as operators automatically. Sometimes this is not what you want. If you want to write W\<emph\>*\</emph\> (a letter with a superscripted asterisk), the asterisk will be interpreted as a multiplication operator. Enclose the direct text within double quotes or add spaceholders.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_idN105D0				0	uz	Examples:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id521866				0	uz	An imported MathType formula contains the following string				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id9413739				0	uz	W rSup { size 8{*} }				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id5988952				0	uz	Change {*} to {} * {} as in the following formula:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id3304544				0	uz	W rSup { size 8{} * {} }				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id4941557				0	uz	You can also use W^"*" to enter the character as direct text.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id9961851				0	uz	Some formulas start with an = sign. Use "=" to enter that character as direct text.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	tit				0	uz	Manually Aligning Formula Parts				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	bm_id3156384				0	uz	\<bookmark_value\>aligning; characters in %PRODUCTNAME Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formula parts; manually aligning\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156384				0	uz	\<variable id=\"align\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/align.xhp\" name=\"Manually Aligning Formula Parts\"\>Manually Aligning Formula Parts\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154657				0	uz	How do you align characters in $[officename] Math quickly and easily?				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	par_id3150249				0	uz	To accomplish this, you must define empty groups and character strings. They do not require any space, but carry information that helps in the alignment process.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	par_id3153912				0	uz	To create empty groups, enter curly brackets \<emph\>{}\</emph\> in the Commands window. In the following example, the goal is to achieve a line break so that the plus signs are beneath one another, even though one less character is entered in the upper line:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	par_id3153246				0	uz	a+a+a+{} newline {}{}{}{}{}a+a+a+a				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	par_id3143229				0	uz	Empty character strings are a simple way to ensure that texts and formulas are left-aligned. They are defined using double inverted commas "" . Make sure you do not use any typographic inverted commas. Example:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	par_id3153809				0	uz	"A further example." newline a+b newline ""c-d				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\main.xhp	0	help	tit				0	uz	Instructions for Using $[officename] Math				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\main.xhp	0	help	bm_id3147341				0	uz	\<bookmark_value\>$[officename] Math;general instructions\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>instructions; $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>Equation Editor, see $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\main.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147341				0	uz	\<variable id=\"main\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/main.xhp\" name=\"Instructions for Using $[officename] Math\"\>Instructions for Using $[officename] Math\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\main.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150199				0	uz	Entering and Editing Formulas				2002-02-02 02:02:02
