helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	hd_id9881893				0	uz	How can I define the limits in a Sum or Integral formula?				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id6504409				0	uz	You want to insert a summation formula like "summation of s^k from k = 0 to n" at the cursor in a Writer text document.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id8811304				0	uz	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Insert - Object - Formula\</item\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id9641712				0	uz	To enable lower and upper limits, click additionally the \<emph\>Upper and Lower Limits\</emph\> icon.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id3304239				0	uz	In the input window, the first placeholder or marker is selected, and you can start to enter the lower limit: 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id8679158				0	uz	k = 0				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id8471327				0	uz	Press F4 to advance to the next marker, and enter the upper limit:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id4731894				0	uz	n				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id759300				0	uz	Press F4 to advance to the next marker, and enter the summand:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id651875				0	uz	s^k				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id6756187				0	uz	Now the formula is complete. Click into your text document outside the formula to leave the formula editor.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id5866267				0	uz	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Insert - Object - Formula\</item\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id1965697				0	uz	Click in the input window and enter the following line:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id9004486				0	uz	int from{a} to{b} f(x)`dx				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id3877071				0	uz	If you don't like the font of the letters f and x, choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Format - Fonts\</item\> and select other fonts. Click the \<emph\>Default\</emph\> button to use the new fonts as default from now on.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id3021332				0	uz	If you need the formula within a line of text, the limits increase the line height. You can choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Format - Text Mode\</item\> to place the limits besides the Sum or Integral symbol, which reduces the line height.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id260322				0	uz	\<link href=\"text/smath/01/03090909.xhp\"\>Example of Integral and Sum ranges\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
