helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150398				0	uz	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03102101.xhp\" name=\"ReDim Statement [Runtime]\"\>ReDim Statement [Runtime]\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3154685				0	uz	Declares a variable or an array.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154218				0	uz	Syntax:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3156214				0	uz	[ReDim]Dim VarName [(start To end)] [As VarType][, VarName2 [(start To end)] [As VarType][,...]]				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id711996				0	uz	Optionally, you can add the \<emph\>Preserve\</emph\> keyword as a parameter to preserve the contents of the array that is redimensioned.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148451				0	uz	Parameters:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3156423				0	uz	\<emph\>VarName:\</emph\> Any variable or array name.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3149562				0	uz	\<emph\>Start, End:\</emph\> Numerical values or constants that define the number of elements (NumberElements=(end-start)+1) and the index range.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3155307				0	uz	Start and End can be numeric expressions if ReDim is used at the procedure level.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3153951				0	uz	\<emph\>VarType:\</emph\> Keyword that declares the data type of a variable.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3147317				0	uz	\<emph\>Keyword:\</emph\> Variable type				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3153728	11			0	uz	\<emph\>Bool: \</emph\>Boolean variable (True, False)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3146121				0	uz	\<emph\>Date:\</emph\> Date variable				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3159156				0	uz	\<emph\>Double:\</emph\> Double floating point variable (1.79769313486232x10E308 - 4.94065645841247x10E-324)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3148616				0	uz	\<emph\>Integer:\</emph\> Integer variable (-32768 - 32767)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3147348				0	uz	\<emph\>Long:\</emph\> Long integer variable (-2,147,483,648 - 2,147,483,647)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3149412				0	uz	\<emph\>Object:\</emph\> Object variable (can only be subsequently defined by Set!)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3154729				0	uz	\<emph\>[Single]:\</emph\> Single floating-point variable (3.402823x10E38 - 1.401298x10E-45). If no key word is specified, a variable is defined as Single, unless a statement from DefBool to DefVar is used.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3148458				0	uz	\<emph\>String:\</emph\> String variable containing a maximum of 64,000 ASCII characters.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3149581	19			0	uz	\<emph\>Variant: \</emph\>Variant variable type (can contain all types and is set by definition).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3155601				0	uz	In $[officename] Basic, you do not need to declare variables explicitly. However, you need to declare an array before you can use them. You can declare a variable with the Dim statement, using commas to separate multiple declarations. To declare a variable type, enter a type-declaration character following the name or use a corresponding key word.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3153415				0	uz	$[officename] Basic supports single or multi-dimensional arrays that are defined by a specified variable type. Arrays are suitable if the program contains lists or tables that you want to edit. The advantage of arrays is that it is possible to address individual elements according to indexes, which can be formulated as numeric expressions or variables.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3146971				0	uz	There are two ways to set the range of indices for arrays declared with the Dim statement:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3153950				0	uz	DIM text(20) As String REM 21 elements numbered from 0 to 20				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3146912				0	uz	DIM text(5 to 25) As String REM 21 elements numbered from 5 to 25				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3153709				0	uz	DIM text$(-15 to 5) As String REM 21 elements (0 inclusive),				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3150321				0	uz	rem numbered from -15 to 5				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3149018				0	uz	Variable fields, regardless of type, can be made dynamic if they are dimensioned by ReDim at the procedure level in subroutines or functions. Normally, you can only set the range of an array once and you cannot modify it. Within a procedure, you can declare an array using the ReDim statement with numeric expressions to define the range of the field sizes.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148405				0	uz	Example:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3154362				0	uz	Sub ExampleRedim				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03102101.xhp	0	help	par_id3150042				0	uz	Dim iVar() As Integer, iCount As Integer				2002-02-02 02:02:02
