helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120308.xhp	0	help	par_id3154924				0	uz	\<emph\>Text\</emph\>: String that you want to right-align in the string variable.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120308.xhp	0	help	par_id3149456				0	uz	\<emph\>Variable1:\</emph\> User-defined variable that is the target for the copied variable.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120308.xhp	0	help	par_id3153381				0	uz	\<emph\>Variable2:\</emph\> User-defined variable that you want to copy to another variable.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120308.xhp	0	help	par_id3154140				0	uz	If the string is shorter than the string variable, \<emph\>RSet\</emph\> aligns the string to the right within the string variable. Any remaining characters in the string variable are replaced with spaces. If the string is longer than the string variable, characters exceeding the length of the variable are truncated, and only the remaining characters are right-aligned within the string variable.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120308.xhp	0	help	par_id3149202				0	uz	You can also use the \<emph\>RSet statement\</emph\> to assign variables of one user-defined type to another.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120308.xhp	0	help	par_id3151042				0	uz	The following example uses the \<emph\>RSet\</emph\> and \<emph\>LSet\</emph\> statements to modify the left and right alignment of a string.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
