helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	tit				0	uz	Format Function [Runtime]				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153539				0	uz	\<bookmark_value\>Format function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153539				0	uz	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03120301.xhp\" name=\"Format Function [Runtime]\"\>Format Function [Runtime]\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3156042				0	uz	Converts a number to a string, and then formats it according to the format that you specify.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145090				0	uz	Syntax:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153527				0	uz	Format (Number [, Format As String])				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149178				0	uz	Return value:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3148474				0	uz	String				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159176				0	uz	Parameters:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3149415				0	uz	\<emph\>Number:\</emph\> Numeric expression that you want to convert to a formatted string.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3147531				0	uz	\<emph\>Format:\</emph\> String that specifies the format code for the number. If \<emph\>Format\</emph\> is omitted, the Format function works like the \<emph\>Str\</emph\> function.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147561				0	uz	Formatting Codes				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3147265				0	uz	The following list describes the codes that you can use for formatting a number:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153380				0	uz	\<emph\>0:\</emph\> If \<emph\>Number\</emph\> has a digit at the position of the 0 in the format code, the digit is displayed, otherwise a zero is displayed.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3151210				0	uz	If \<emph\>Number\</emph\> has fewer digits than the number of zeros in the format code, (on either side of the decimal), leading or trailing zeros are displayed. If the number has more digits to the left of the decimal separator than the amount of zeros in the format code, the additional digits are displayed without formatting.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3151176	14			0	uz	Decimal places in the number are rounded according to the number of zeros that appear after the decimal separator in the \<emph\>Format \</emph\>code.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3154123				0	uz	\<emph\>#:\</emph\> If \<emph\>Number\</emph\> contains a digit at the position of the # placeholder in the \<emph\>Format\</emph\> code, the digit is displayed, otherwise nothing is displayed at this position.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3148452	16			0	uz	This symbol works like the 0, except that leading or trailing zeroes are not displayed if there are more # characters in the format code than digits in the number. Only the relevant digits of the number are displayed.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3159150				0	uz	\<emph\>.:\</emph\> The decimal placeholder determines the number of decimal places to the left and right of the decimal separator.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3159252				0	uz	If the format code contains only # placeholders to the left of this symbol, numbers less than 1 begin with a decimal separator. To always display a leading zero with fractional numbers, use 0 as a placeholder for the first digit to the left of the decimal separator.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153368				0	uz	\<emph\>%:\</emph\> Multiplies the number by 100 and inserts the percent sign (%) where the number appears in the format code.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3149481				0	uz	\<emph\>E- E+ e- e+ :\</emph\> If the format code contains at least one digit placeholder (0 or #) to the right of the symbol E-, E+, e-, or e+, the number is formatted in the scientific or exponential format. The letter E or e is inserted between the number and the exponent. The number of placeholders for digits to the right of the symbol determines the number of digits in the exponent.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3149262				0	uz	If the exponent is negative, a minus sign is displayed directly before an exponent with E-, E+, e-, e+. If the exponent is positive, a plus sign is only displayed before exponents with E+ or e+.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3148617				0	uz	The thousands delimiter is displayed if the format code contains the delimiter enclosed by digit placeholders (0 or #).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3163713				0	uz	The use of a period as a thousands and decimal separator is dependent on the regional setting. When you enter a number directly in Basic source code, always use a period as decimal delimiter. The actual character displayed as a decimal separator depends on the number format in your system settings.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3152887				0	uz	\<emph\>- + $ ( ) space:\</emph\> A plus (+), minus (-), dollar ($), space, or brackets entered directly in the format code is displayed as a literal character.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3148576				0	uz	To display characters other than the ones listed here, you must precede it by a backslash (\\), or enclose it in quotation marks (" ").				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153139				0	uz	\\ : The backslash displays the next character in the format code.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153366				0	uz	Characters in the format code that have a special meaning can only be displayed as literal characters if they are preceded by a backslash. The backslash itself is not displayed, unless you enter a double backslash (\\\\) in the format code.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3155411				0	uz	Characters that must be preceded by a backslash in the format code in order to be displayed as literal characters are date- and time-formatting characters (a, c, d, h, m, n, p, q, s, t, w, y, /, :), numeric-formatting characters (#, 0, %, E, e, comma, period), and string-formatting characters (@, &, <, >, !).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3145749				0	uz	You can also use the following predefined number formats. Except for "General Number", all of the predefined format codes return the number as a decimal number with two decimal places.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3150113				0	uz	If you use predefined formats, the name of the format must be enclosed in quotation marks.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149377				0	uz	Predefined format				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3154730				0	uz	\<emph\>General Number:\</emph\> Numbers are displayed as entered.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153158				0	uz	\<emph\>Currency:\</emph\> Inserts a dollar sign in front of the number and encloses negative numbers in brackets.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3154490				0	uz	\<emph\>Fixed:\</emph\> Displays at least one digit in front of the decimal separator.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153415				0	uz	\<emph\>Standard:\</emph\> Displays numbers with a thousands separator.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3150715				0	uz	\<emph\>Percent:\</emph\> Multiplies the number by 100 and appends a percent sign to the number.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153836				0	uz	\<emph\>Scientific:\</emph\> Displays numbers in scientific format (for example, 1.00E+03 for 1000).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153707				0	uz	A format code can be divided into three sections that are separated by semicolons. The first part defines the format for positive values, the second part for negative values, and the third part for zero. If you only specify one format code, it applies to all numbers.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149019				0	uz	Example:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3156054				0	uz	Sub ExampleFormat				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3148993				0	uz	MsgBox Format(6328.2, "##,##0.00")				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_idN107A2				0	uz	REM always use a period as decimal delimiter when you enter numbers in Basic source code.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3147339				0	uz	REM displays for example 6,328.20 in English locale, 6.328,20 in German locale.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3156382				0	uz	End Sub				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	tit				0	uz	Dir Function [Runtime]				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154347				0	uz	\<bookmark_value\>Dir function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154347				0	uz	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03020404.xhp\" name=\"Dir Function [Runtime]\"\>Dir Function [Runtime]\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3153381				0	uz	Returns the name of a file, a directory, or all of the files and the directories on a drive or in a directory that match the specified search path.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154365				0	uz	Syntax:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3156282				0	uz	Dir [(Text As String) [, Attrib As Integer]]				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156424				0	uz	Return value:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3153193				0	uz	String				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153770				0	uz	Parameters:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3161831				0	uz	\<emph\>Text:\</emph\> Any string expression that specifies the search path, directory or file. This argument can only be specified the first time that you call the Dir function. If you want, you can enter the path in \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/00000002.xhp\" name=\"URL notation\"\>URL notation\</link\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3146974	9			0	uz	\<emph\>Attrib: \</emph\>Any integer expression that specifies bitwise file attributes. The Dir function only returns files or directories that match the specified attributes. You can combine several attributes by adding the attribute values:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3149666				0	uz	0 : Normal files.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3147427				0	uz	16 : Returns the name of the directory only.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3153952				0	uz	Use this attribute to check if a file or directory exists, or to determine all files and folders in a specific directory.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3159156				0	uz	To check if a file exists, enter the complete path and name of the file. If the file or directory name does not exist, the Dir function returns a zero-length string ("").				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3154012	18			0	uz	To generate a list of all existing files in a specific directory, proceed as follows: The first time you call the Dir function, specify the complete search path for the files, for example, "D:\\Files\\*.sxw". If the path is correct and the search finds at least one file, the Dir function returns the name of the first file that matches the search path. To return additional file names that match the path, call Dir again, but with no arguments.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3147348				0	uz	To return directories only, use the attribute parameter. The same applies if you want to determine the name of a volume (for example, a hard drive partition)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154942				0	uz	Example:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3147125				0	uz	Sub ExampleDir				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3148455				0	uz	REM Displays all files and directories				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3147396				0	uz	Dim sPath As String				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3149378				0	uz	Dim sDir as String, sValue as String				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3153416				0	uz	sDir="Directories:"				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3153838				0	uz	sPath = CurDir				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3150327				0	uz	sValue = Dir$(sPath + getPathSeparator + "*",16)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3155064				0	uz	Do				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3153764				0	uz	If sValue <> "." and sValue <> ".." Then				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3155766				0	uz	if (GetAttr( sPath + getPathSeparator + sValue) AND 16) >0 then				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3154253				0	uz	REM get the directories				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3159264				0	uz	sDir = sDir & chr(13) & sValue				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3145148				0	uz	End If				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_idN10700				0	uz	End If				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3147324				0	uz	sValue = Dir$				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3155335				0	uz	Loop Until sValue = ""				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3147345				0	uz	MsgBox sDir,0,sPath				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020404.xhp	0	help	par_id3163808				0	uz	End sub				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03010103.xhp	0	help	tit				0	uz	Print Statement [Runtime]				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03010103.xhp	0	help	bm_id3147230				0	uz	\<bookmark_value\>Print statement\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03010103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147230				0	uz	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03010103.xhp\" name=\"Print Statement [Runtime]\"\>Print Statement [Runtime]\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03010103.xhp	0	help	par_id3156281				0	uz	Outputs the specified strings or numeric expressions to a dialog or to a file.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03010103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145785				0	uz	Syntax:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03010103.xhp	0	help	par_id3153188	4			0	uz	Print [#FileName,] Expression1[{;|,} [Spc(Number As Integer);] [Tab(pos As Integer);] [Expression2[...]] 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03010103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147348				0	uz	Parameter:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03010103.xhp	0	help	par_id2508621				0	uz	\<emph\>FileName:\</emph\> Any numeric expression that contains the file number that was set by the Open statement for the respective file.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03010103.xhp	0	help	par_id3163712				0	uz	\<emph\>Expression\</emph\>: Any numeric or string expression to be printed. Multiple expressions can be separated by a semicolon. If separated by a comma, the expressions are indented to the next tab stop. The tab stops cannot be adjusted.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03010103.xhp	0	help	par_id3153092				0	uz	\<emph\>Number\</emph\>: Number of spaces to be inserted by the \<emph\>Spc\</emph\> function.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03010103.xhp	0	help	par_id3145364				0	uz	\<emph\>Pos\</emph\>: Spaces are inserted until the specified position.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03010103.xhp	0	help	par_id3154319				0	uz	If a semicolon or comma appears after the last expression to be printed, $[officename] Basic stores the text in an internal buffer and continues program execution without printing. When another Print statement without a semicolon or comma at the end is encountered, all text to be printed is printed at once.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03010103.xhp	0	help	par_id3145272				0	uz	Positive numeric expressions are printed with a leading space. Negative expressions are printed with a leading minus sign. If a certain range is exceeded for floating-point values, the respective numeric expression is printed in exponential notation.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03010103.xhp	0	help	par_id3154011				0	uz	If the expression to be printed exceeds a certain length, the display will automatically wrap to the next line.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03010103.xhp	0	help	par_id3146969				0	uz	You can insert the Tab function, enclosed by semicolons, between arguments to indent the output to a specific position, or you can use the \<emph\>Spc\</emph\> function to insert a specified number of spaces.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03010103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146912				0	uz	Example:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03010103.xhp	0	help	par_id3153711				0	uz	Sub ExamplePrint				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03010103.xhp	0	help	par_id3153764				0	uz	Print "ABC"				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03010103.xhp	0	help	par_id3155764				0	uz	Print "ABC","123"				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03010103.xhp	0	help	par_id5484176				0	uz	i = FreeFile()				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03010103.xhp	0	help	par_id2904141				0	uz	Open \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"WIN\"\>"C:\\Temp.txt"\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>"~/temp.txt"\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> For Output As i				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03010103.xhp	0	help	par_id36317				0	uz	Print #i, "ABC"				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03010103.xhp	0	help	par_id7381817				0	uz	Close #i				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03010103.xhp	0	help	par_id3147339				0	uz	end Sub				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\00000002.xhp	0	help	tit				0	uz	$[officename] Basic Glossary				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145068				0	uz	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/00000002.xhp\" name=\"$[officename] Basic Glossary\"\>$[officename] Basic Glossary\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3150792				0	uz	This glossary explains some technical terms that you may come across when working with $[officename] Basic.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155133				0	uz	Decimal Point				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3156443				0	uz	When converting numbers, $[officename] Basic uses the locale settings of the system for determining the type of decimal and thousand separator.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3153092				0	uz	The behavior has an effect on both the implicit conversion ( 1 + "2.3" = 3.3 ) as well as the runtime function \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03102700.xhp\" name=\"IsNumeric\"\>IsNumeric\</link\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155854				0	uz	Colors				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3145366				0	uz	In $[officename] Basic, colors are treated as long integer value. The return value of color queries is also always a long integer value. When defining properties, colors can be specified using their RGB code that is converted to a long integer value using the \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03010305.xhp\" name=\"RGB function\"\>RGB function\</link\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146119				0	uz	Measurement Units				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\00000002.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145801				0	uz	\<bookmark_value\>twips; definition\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145801				0	uz	Twips				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3154731				0	uz	A twip is a screen-independent unit which is used to define the uniform position and size of screen elements on all display systems. A twip is 1/1440th of an inch or 1/20 of a printer's point. There are 1440 twips to an inch or about 567 twips to a centimeter.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\00000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153159				0	uz	URL Notation				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3153415				0	uz	URLs (\<emph\>Uniform Resource Locators\</emph\>) are used to determine the location of a resource like a file in a file system, typically inside a network environment. A URL consists of a protocol specifier, a host specifier and a file and path specifier:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3149121				0	uz	\<emph\>protocol\</emph\>://\<emph\>host.name\</emph\>/\<emph\>path/to/the/file.html\</emph\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3168612				0	uz	The most common usage of URLs is on the internet when specifying web pages. Example for protocols are \<emph\>http\</emph\>, \<emph\>ftp\</emph\>, or \<emph\>file\</emph\>. The \<emph\>file\</emph\> protocol specifier is used when referring to a file on the local file system.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\00000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3150324	110			0	uz	URL notation does not allow certain special characters to be used. These are either replaced by other characters or encoded. A slash (\<emph\>/\</emph\>) is used as a path separator. For example, a file referred to as \<emph\>C:\\My File.sxw\</emph\> on the local host in "Windows notation" becomes \<emph\>file:///C|/My%20File.sxw\</emph\> in URL notation.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020415.xhp	0	help	tit				0	uz	FileExists Function [Runtime]				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020415.xhp	0	help	bm_id3148946				0	uz	\<bookmark_value\>FileExists function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020415.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148946				0	uz	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03020415.xhp\" name=\"FileExists Function [Runtime]\"\>FileExists Function [Runtime]\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020415.xhp	0	help	par_id3153361				0	uz	Determines if a file or a directory is available on the data medium.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020415.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150447				0	uz	Syntax:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020415.xhp	0	help	par_id3154685				0	uz	FileExists(FileName As String | DirectoryName As String)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020415.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154126				0	uz	Return value:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020415.xhp	0	help	par_id3150769				0	uz	Bool				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020415.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153770				0	uz	Parameters:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020415.xhp	0	help	par_id3147349				0	uz	FileName | DirectoryName: Any string expression that contains an unambiguous file specification. You can also use \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/00000002.xhp\" name=\"URL notation\"\>URL notation\</link\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020415.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149664				0	uz	Example:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020415.xhp	0	help	par_id3145272				0	uz	sub ExampleFileExists				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020415.xhp	0	help	par_id3147317				0	uz	msgbox FileExists("C:\\autoexec.bat")				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020415.xhp	0	help	par_id3153190				0	uz	msgbox FileExists("file:///d|/bookmark.htm")				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020415.xhp	0	help	par_id3148645				0	uz	msgbox FileExists("file:///d|/private")				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03020415.xhp	0	help	par_id3149262				0	uz	end sub				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	tit				0	uz	Cos Function [Runtime]				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154923				0	uz	\<bookmark_value\>Cos function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154923				0	uz	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03080102.xhp\" name=\"Cos Function [Runtime]\"\>Cos Function [Runtime]\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	par_id3159413				0	uz	Calculates the cosine of an angle. The angle is specified in radians. The result lies between -1 and 1.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	par_id3150358				0	uz	Using the angle Alpha, the Cos-Function calculates the ratio of the length of the side that is adjacent to the angle, divided by the length of the hypotenuse in a right-angled triangle.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	par_id3154141				0	uz	Cos(Alpha) = Adjacent/Hypotenuse				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154125				0	uz	Syntax:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	par_id3145172				0	uz	Cos (Number)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156214				0	uz	Return value:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	par_id3150449				0	uz	Double				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153969				0	uz	Parameters:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	par_id3153770				0	uz	\<emph\>Number:\</emph\> Numeric expression that specifies an angle in radians that you want to calculate the cosine for.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	par_id3145749				0	uz	To convert degrees to radians, multiply degrees by pi/180. To convert radians to degrees, multiply radians by 180/pi.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	par_id3149664				0	uz	degree=(radian*180)/pi				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	par_id3146985				0	uz	radian=(degree*pi)/180				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	par_id3152885				0	uz	Pi is here the fixed circle constant with the rounded value 3.14159...				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153951				0	uz	Example:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	par_id3155855				0	uz	REM The following example allows for a right-angled triangle the input of				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	par_id3149484				0	uz	REM secant and angle (in degrees) and calculates the length of the hypotenuse:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
