helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145645				0	uz	\<variable id=\"01020200\"\>\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01020200.xhp\"\>Using the Object Catalog\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153707				0	uz	The object catalog provides an overview of all modules and dialogs you have created in $[officename].				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147346	78			0	uz	Click the \<emph\>Object Catalog\</emph\> icon \<image id=\"img_id3147341\" src=\"res/commandimagelist/sc_objectcatalog.png\" width=\"0.564cm\" height=\"0.564cm\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3147341\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\> in the Macro toolbar to display the object catalog.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155114				0	uz	The dialog shows a list of all existing objects in a hierarchical representation. Double-clicking a list entry opens its subordinate objects.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150786	83			0	uz	To display a certain module in the Editor or to position the cursor in a selected SUB or FUNCTION, select the corresponding entry and click the \<emph\>Show\</emph\> icon \<image id=\"img_id3149527\" src=\"basctl/res/im01.png\" width=\"0.564cm\" height=\"0.564cm\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3149527\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153266				0	uz	Click the (X) icon in the title bar to close the object catalog.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	tit				0	uz	For...Next Statement [Runtime]				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149205				0	uz	\<bookmark_value\>For statement\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>To statement\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>Step statement\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>Next statement\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149205				0	uz	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03090202.xhp\" name=\"For...Next Statement [Runtime]\"\>For...Next Statement [Runtime]\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	par_id3143267				0	uz	Repeats the statements between the For...Next block a specified number of times.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156153				0	uz	Syntax:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	par_id3148473				0	uz	For counter=start To end [Step step]				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	par_id3156024				0	uz	statement block				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	par_id3146796				0	uz	[Exit For]				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	par_id3159414				0	uz	statement block				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	par_id3153897				0	uz	Next [counter]				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150400				0	uz	Variables:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	par_id3150358				0	uz	\<emph\>Counter:\</emph\> Loop counter initially assigned the value to the right of the equal sign (start). Only numeric variables are valid. The loop counter increases or decreases according to the variable Step until End is passed.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	par_id3152455				0	uz	\<emph\>Start:\</emph\> Numeric variable that defines the initial value at the beginning of the loop.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	par_id3151043				0	uz	\<emph\>End:\</emph\> Numeric variable that defines the final value at the end of the loop.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	par_id3156281				0	uz	\<emph\>Step:\</emph\> Sets the value by which to increase or decrease the loop counter. If Step is not specified, the loop counter is incremented by 1. In this case, End must be greater than Start. If you want to decrease Counter, End must be less than Start, and Step must be assigned a negative value.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	par_id3154684				0	uz	The \<emph\>For...Next\</emph\> loop repeats all of the statements in the loop for the number of times that is specified by the parameters.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	par_id3147287				0	uz	As the counter variable is decreased, $[officename] Basic checks if the end value has been reached. As soon as the counter passes the end value, the loop automatically ends.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	par_id3159154				0	uz	It is possible to nest \<emph\>For...Next\</emph\> statements. If you do not specify a variable following the \<emph\>Next\</emph\> statement, \<emph\>Next\</emph\> automatically refers to the most recent \<emph\>For\</emph\> statement.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	par_id3155306				0	uz	If you specify an increment of 0, the statements between \<emph\>For\</emph\> and \<emph\>Next\</emph\> are repeated continuously.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	par_id3155854				0	uz	When counting down the counter variable, $[officename] Basic checks for overflow or underflow. The loop ends when Counter exceeds End (positive Step value) or is less than End (negative Step value).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	par_id3145273				0	uz	Use the \<emph\>Exit For\</emph\> statement to exit the loop unconditionally. This statement must be within a \<emph\>For...Next\</emph\> loop. Use the \<emph\>If...Then\</emph\> statement to test the exit condition as follows:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	par_id3153190				0	uz	For...				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	par_id3149482				0	uz	statements				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	par_id3147124				0	uz	If condition = True Then Exit For				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	par_id3153159				0	uz	statements				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	par_id3154096				0	uz	Next				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	par_id3156286				0	uz	Note: In nested \<emph\>For...Next\</emph\> loops, if you exit a loop unconditionally with \<emph\>Exit For\</emph\>, only one loop is exited.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148457				0	uz	Example				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090202.xhp	0	help	par_id3151074				0	uz	The following example uses two nested loops to sort a string array with 10 elements ( sEntry() ), that are first filled with various contents:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
