helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id3004547				0	uz	The open, low, high, and closing values of a row build together one data unit in the chart. A stock price data series consists of several rows containing such data units. The column containing the transaction volume builds an optional second data series.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id6401867				0	uz	Depending on the chosen variant, you do not need all columns.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	hd_id18616				0	uz	Stock Chart Variants				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id6138492				0	uz	Choose the \<emph\>Stock\</emph\> chart type on the first page of the \<link href=\"text/schart/01/wiz_chart_type.xhp\"\>Chart wizard\</link\>. Then select one of the four variants.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	hd_id4569231				0	uz	Type 1				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id291451				0	uz	Based on\<emph\> low\</emph\>\<emph\>and high \</emph\>column the Type 1 shows the distance between bottom price (low) and top price (high) by a vertical line.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id3341776				0	uz	Based on\<emph\> low, high,\</emph\> and \<emph\>close\</emph\> column Type 1 shows an additional horizontal mark \<emph\>for\</emph\> the closing price.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	hd_id5947141				0	uz	Type 2				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id1911679				0	uz	Based on \<emph\>open, low, high\</emph\>, and \<emph\>close\</emph\> column Type 2 generates the traditional "candle stick" chart. Type 2 draws the vertical line between the bottom and top price and adds a rectangle in front, which visualizes the range between the opening and closing price. If you click on the rectangle you see more information in the status bar. %PRODUCTNAME uses different fill colors for rising values (the opening price is lower than the closing price) and falling values.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	hd_id9364909				0	uz	Type 3				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id4473403				0	uz	Based on \<emph\>volume, low, high\</emph\>, and \<emph\>close\</emph\> column chart Type 3 draws a chart like Type 1, with additional columns for the transaction volume.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	hd_id4313791				0	uz	Type 4				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id4331797				0	uz	Based on all five data columns \<emph\>volume, open, low, high\</emph\>, and \<emph\>close\</emph\>, Type 4 combines a chart of Type 2 with a column chart for the transaction volume.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id4191717				0	uz	Because measurement for transaction volume might be "units", a second y axis is introduced in chart Type 3 and Type 4. The price axis is shown on the right side and the volume axis on the left side.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	hd_id2318796				0	uz	Setting the Data Source				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	hd_id399182				0	uz	Charts based on its own data				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id5298318				0	uz	To change the data series of a chart having its own data, choose \<link href=\"text/schart/01/03010000.xhp\"\>Chart Data Table\</link\> from the View menu or from the context menu of the chart in edit mode.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id7588732				0	uz	In an embedded chart data table, the data series are always organized in columns.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id95828				0	uz	For a new stock chart first use a column chart. Add the columns you need and enter your data in the order which is shown in the example, omitting any columns not required for the desired variant. Use Move Series Right to change the column order. Close the chart data table. Now use the Chart Type dialog to change to the stock chart variant. 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id6182744				0	uz	If you have already got a stock chart and you want to change the variant, then first change the chart type to a column chart, add or remove columns so that it fits to the variant, and then change the chart type back to a stock chart.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id3496200				0	uz	Do not write the name of a data series in a row. Write the name into the field above the role name.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id7599108				0	uz	The order of the rows determines how the categories are arranged in the chart. Use Move Row Down to change the order.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	hd_id888698				0	uz	Charts based on Calc or Writer tables				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id3394573				0	uz	You can choose or alter a data range on the second page of the Chart wizard or in the \<link href=\"text/schart/01/wiz_data_range.xhp\"\>Data Range\</link\> dialog. For fine tuning use the \<link href=\"text/schart/01/wiz_data_series.xhp\"\>Data Series\</link\> dialog.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id7594225				0	uz	To specify a data range do one of the following:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id5081637				0	uz	Enter the data range in the text box.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id9759514				0	uz	In Calc, an example data range would be "$Sheet1.$B$3:$B$14". Note that a data range may consist of more than one region in a spreadsheet, e.g. "$Sheet1.A1:A5;$Sheet1.D1:D5" is also a valid data range. In Writer, an example data range would be "Table1.A1:E4".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id1614429				0	uz	As long as the syntax is not correct, %PRODUCTNAME shows the text in red.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id1589098				0	uz	In Calc, click \<emph\>Select data range\</emph\> to minimize the dialog, then drag to select the data range. When you release the mouse, the data are entered. Click \<emph\>Select data range\</emph\> again to add a data range. In the input field of the minimized dialog, click after the entry and type a semicolon. Then drag to select the next range.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id8746910				0	uz	Click one of the options for data series in rows or in columns. 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id9636524				0	uz	Your stock chart data are "in columns", if the information in a row belongs to the same "candle stick".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	hd_id5675527				0	uz	Fine Tuning the Data Ranges of Table Based Stock Charts				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id3486434				0	uz	You can organize data series and edit the source for parts of single data series on the third page of the Chart wizard or on the page Data Series in the Data Range dialog.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	hd_id3068636				0	uz	Organize Data Series				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id2480849				0	uz	In the \<emph\>data series\</emph\> area on the left side of the dialog, you can organize the data series of the actual chart. A stock chart has at least one data series containing the prices. It might have a second data series for transaction volume.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id4181951				0	uz	If you have got more than one price data series, use the Up and Down arrow buttons to order them. The order determines the arrangement in the chart. Do the same for volume data series. You cannot switch price and volume data series.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id2927335				0	uz	To remove a data series, select the data series in the list and click \<emph\>Remove\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id2107303				0	uz	To add a data series, select one of the existing data series and click \<emph\>Add\</emph\>. You get an empty entry below the selected one, which has the same type. If you have no price data series or no volume data series, you must first select a range for these series in the \<emph\>Data Range\</emph\> dialog.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	hd_id4071779				0	uz	Setting Data Ranges				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id7844477				0	uz	In the \<emph\>Data Ranges\</emph\> dialog you can set or change the data range of each component of the selected data series.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id6478469				0	uz	In the upper list you see the role name of the components and the current values. When you have selected a role, you can change the value in the text box below the list. The label shows the selected role.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id9038972				0	uz	Enter the range into the text box or click on \<emph\>Select data range\</emph\> to minimize the dialog and select the range with the mouse.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id7985168				0	uz	Select Open Values, Close Values, High Values, and Low Values in any order. Specify only the ranges for those roles which you need for the chosen variant of the stock chart. The ranges need not be next to each other in the table.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	hd_id876186				0	uz	Legend				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id3939634				0	uz	The legend displays the labels from the first row or column or from the special range that you have set in the \<emph\>Data Series\</emph\> dialog. If your chart does not contain labels, the legend displays text like "Row 1, Row 2, ...", or "Column A, Column B, ..." according to the row number or column letter of the chart data.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id2377697				0	uz	The legend shows the value from the range, which you entered in the \<emph\>Range for Name\</emph\> field in the \<emph\>Data Range\</emph\> dialog. The default entry is the column header of the closing price column.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\type_stock.xhp	0	help	par_id2188787				0	uz	Select one of the position options. When the chart is finished, you can specify other positions using the Format menu.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\05040201.xhp	0	help	tit				0	uz	Scale				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\05040201.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150868				0	uz	\<bookmark_value\>scaling; axes\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>logarithmic scaling along axes\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>charts;scaling axes\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>X axes;scaling\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>Y axes; scaling\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\05040201.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150868				0	uz	\<link href=\"text/schart/01/05040201.xhp\" name=\"Scale\"\>Scale\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\05040201.xhp	0	help	par_id3149379				0	uz	To achieve specific results, you can manually change the axis scaling. For example, you can display only the top areas of the columns by shifting the zero line upwards.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\05040201.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154730				0	uz	Scale				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\05040201.xhp	0	help	par_id3149400				0	uz	You can enter values for subdividing axes in this area. You can automatically set the properties \<emph\>Minimum, Maximum, Major interval, Minor interval count\</emph\> and \<emph\>Reference value\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\05040201.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150751				0	uz	Minimum				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\05040201.xhp	0	help	par_id3153713				0	uz	\<ahelp hid=\"SCH_SPINFIELD_TP_SCALE_EDT_MIN\"\>Defines the minimum value for the beginning of the axis.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\05040201.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156385				0	uz	Maximum				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\05040201.xhp	0	help	par_id3159266				0	uz	\<ahelp hid=\"SCH_SPINFIELD_TP_SCALE_EDT_MAX\"\>Defines the maximum value for the end of the axis.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\05040201.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155336				0	uz	Major interval				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\05040201.xhp	0	help	par_id3143218				0	uz	\<ahelp hid=\"SCH_SPINFIELD_TP_SCALE_EDT_STEP_MAIN\"\>Defines the interval for the main division of the axes.\</ahelp\> The main interval cannot be larger than the value area.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\05040201.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154020				0	uz	Minor interval count				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\05040201.xhp	0	help	par_id3154656				0	uz	\<ahelp hid=\"SCH_SPINFIELD_TP_SCALE_EDT_STEP_HELP\"\>Defines the interval for the subdivision of the axes.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\05040201.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150089				0	uz	Reference value				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\05040201.xhp	0	help	par_id3152990				0	uz	\<ahelp hid=\"SCH_SPINFIELD_TP_SCALE_EDT_ORIGIN\"\>Specifies at which position to display the values along the axis.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\05040201.xhp	0	help	hd_id3166432				0	uz	Automatic				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\05040201.xhp	0	help	par_id3145389				0	uz	\<ahelp hid=\"SCH:CHECKBOX:TP_SCALE:CBX_AUTO_ORIGIN\"\>You must first deselect the \<emph\>Automatic\</emph\> option in order to modify the values.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\05040201.xhp	0	help	par_id3149129				0	uz	Disable this feature if you are working with "fixed" values, as it does not permit automatic scaling.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\05040201.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159206				0	uz	Logarithmic scale				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\05040201.xhp	0	help	par_id3145360				0	uz	\<ahelp hid=\"SCH:CHECKBOX:TP_SCALE:CBX_LOGARITHM\"\>Specifies that you want the axis to be subdivided logarithmically.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\05040201.xhp	0	help	par_id3153956				0	uz	Use this feature if you are working with values that differ sharply from each other. You can use logarithmic scaling to make the grid lines of the axis equidistant but have values that may increase or decrease.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\05040201.xhp	0	help	hd_id9941404				0	uz	Reverse direction				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\05040201.xhp	0	help	par_id5581835				0	uz	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Defines where the lower and where the higher values are displayed at the axis. The unchecked state is the mathematical direction.\</ahelp\> That means for Cartesian coordinate systems that the x-axis shows the lower values on the left and the y-axis shows the lower values at the bottom. For polar coordinate systems the mathematical angle axis direction is counterclockwise and the radial axis is from inner to outer.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\choose_chart_type.xhp	0	help	tit				0	uz	Choosing a Chart Type				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\choose_chart_type.xhp	0	help	hd_id9072237				0	uz	\<variable id=\"choose_chart_type\"\>\<link href=\"text/schart/01/choose_chart_type.xhp\"\>Choosing a Chart Type\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\choose_chart_type.xhp	0	help	par_id7085787				0	uz	On the first page of the \<link href=\"text/schart/01/wiz_chart_type.xhp\"\>Chart Wizard\</link\> you can choose a chart type. 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\choose_chart_type.xhp	0	help	hd_id6820886				0	uz	The available chart types				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\schart\01\choose_chart_type.xhp	0	help	par_id7309488				0	uz	Choose from the following chart types, depending on data type and intended presentation effect.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
